# xbindkeysrc — mouse-button bindings only.
#
# WHY xbindkeys is limited to the mouse here: keyboard shortcuts are owned by
# xfce4-keyboard-shortcuts, an xfconf channel that survives package updates and is
# readable with `xfconf-query -c xfce4-keyboard-shortcuts -l`. xbindkeys would
# bind keys at the X server level, and with it running there is no way to ask Xfce
# which bindings exist — the two systems would then both claim the same key and
# the winner would depend on focus timing. So: keys go to the xfconf channel
# (config/xfce/xfce-perchannel-xml/xfce4-keyboard-shortcuts/), buttons go here.
#
# Spec §10 also wants this to keep working if xfwm4's compositor is ever turned
# off. xbindkeys binds at the X server, not in the compositor, so it is
# unaffected either way.
#
# Button numbering in xbindkeys: 1 = left, 2 = middle, 3 = right.

# Maximise vertically. wmctrl is used rather than a synthesised keystroke because
# "maximise vertically" has no default xfwm4 keystroke to forward to; it is only
# reachable as a client message. `-r :ACTIVE:` targets the focused window so this
# works regardless of which window has pointer focus.
"wmctrl -r :ACTIVE: -b add,maximized_vert"
  Super + Button2

# Close window. Forwards the keystroke rather than sending WM_DELETE directly so
# the app's own close handling (unsaved-changes prompts, "close tab" vs "close
# window") runs exactly as it would for the keyboard shortcut.
"xdotool key --clearmodifiers super+q"
  Super + Button3